## 8. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & API DESIGN

### 8.1 Architecture (3-Tier)

```
[Data Sources] → [Backend API] → [Frontend Dashboard]
     │                │                    │
  NFStream        FastAPI             React + Vite
  PCAP files      ML Models           Real-time UI
  Live capture    MongoDB             Recharts
                  Threat Intel        Copilot Chat
                  Consensus Engine
                  Alerting
```

### 8.2 API Endpoints (40+ routes)

**Core:** `/ingest`, `/logs`, `/alerts`, `/stats`
**ML:** `/models/metrics`
**Copilot:** `/copilot/chat`
**Incidents:** `/incidents`, `/reports/{id}`
**Blocklist:** `/blocklist` (CRUD)
**Users:** `/auth/signup`, `/auth/login`, `/users`
**MITRE:** `/alerts/{id}/mitre`, `/alerts/{id}/playbook`
**Forensics:** `/forensics/{ip}`
**Ingestion:** `/capture/start`, `/capture/stop`, `/upload-file`
**Settings:** `/settings`, `/alerts/config`

### 8.3 Data Flow (per ingested flow)

```
1. Raw packet → NFStream → flow features
2. Feature standardization (key normalization to CICIDS format)
3. Blocklist check → DROP if IP is blocked
4. StandardScaler transform
5. Random Forest predict_proba → risk_score
6. Isolation Forest predict → is_anomaly
7. VirusTotal + AbuseIPDB lookup → threat_intel
8. Consensus Engine → unified verdict
9. Attack type classification (rule-based + ML-confidence)
10. Store in MongoDB (logs collection)
11. If threat → create alert, send notifications
```

---

## 9. LIMITATIONS (Be Honest!)

### 9.1 ML Limitations

| Limitation | Impact | Mitigation |
|------------|--------|------------|
| **Trained on CICIDS2017 only** | May miss attacks not represented in training data | Isolation Forest provides zero-day coverage; model can be retrained |
| **Binary classification** | Doesn't identify specific attack sub-types via ML | Rule-based classifier post-ML handles sub-type labeling |
| **Cross-dataset generalization** | 50% accuracy on IDS2018 | Expected — different networks produce different traffic profiles. Retraining required. |
| **Encrypted traffic** | Cannot inspect payload content | Flow-level features (timing, sizes, rates) still reveal behavioral patterns |
| **Infiltration detection** | Low precision (17.7%) | Only 36 training samples — need more data |

### 9.2 System Limitations

| Limitation | Impact | Future Fix |
|------------|--------|------------|
| **Single-server** | No horizontal scaling | Containerize with Docker, add load balancer |
| **Polling-based dashboard** | 2-second refresh delay | Upgrade to WebSockets for true real-time |
| **No authentication middleware** | API endpoints are open | Add JWT-based auth middleware |
| **No rate limiting** | API can be overwhelmed | Add FastAPI middleware or nginx proxy |
| **macOS-only live capture tested** | Linux interface names differ | NFStream supports Linux — just change interface name |
| **No TLS on API** | Traffic between frontend/backend is unencrypted | Add nginx reverse proxy with SSL |

### 9.3 Scalability

| Metric | Current Capacity |
|--------|-----------------|
| **Ingestion throughput** | ~20 flows/second (single API instance) |
| **Database** | MongoDB Atlas M0 = 512MB storage (~500K logs) |
| **Model inference** | ~5ms per flow (RF + IF + scaler) |
| **Concurrent users** | FastAPI handles ~1000 concurrent connections |

**Bottleneck:** Threat Intel API calls (VirusTotal/AbuseIPDB have rate limits — 4 req/min on free tier).
**Fix:** Add caching layer (Redis) for IP reputation lookups.

---

## 10. SECURITY OF THE SYSTEM ITSELF

### 10.1 "What if YOUR system gets attacked?"

| Vulnerability | Risk | Mitigation |
|---------------|------|------------|
| **API has no auth** | Anyone can call `/ingest` and poison the database | Add API key or JWT authentication |
| **MongoDB injection** | Malicious flow data could contain NoSQL injection payloads | Pydantic validation + Motor's parameterized queries prevent injection |
| **Adversarial ML** | Attacker crafts traffic that fools the RF model | Isolation Forest acts as a second opinion; adversarial-resistant by design (tree-based models are harder to attack than neural networks) |
| **Model poisoning** | If attacker controls training data | Models are trained offline on vetted CICIDS2017 dataset — not online learning |
| **DoS on the API** | Flood `/ingest` endpoint | Add rate limiting; NFStream already throttles flow emission |
| **Credential exposure** | `.env` file contains API keys | `.env` is in `.gitignore`; never committed to version control |
| **SSRF via Threat Intel** | IP lookup could be exploited | We only query VirusTotal/AbuseIPDB — trusted external APIs |

### 10.2 "What about adversarial machine learning?"

- **Tree-based models (RF) are inherently more robust** to adversarial perturbations than neural networks.
- An attacker would need to modify flow-level features (packet sizes, timing) in real-time — this is significantly harder than modifying image pixels.
- The **consensus engine** requires fooling BOTH models simultaneously, plus evading Threat Intel — triple evasion is exponentially harder.

---

## 11. OS-SPECIFIC & FORMAT CONSIDERATIONS

### 11.1 "Does it work on Linux/Windows attacks?"

- **The ML models are OS-agnostic** — they analyze network FLOW features (packet sizes, rates, protocols), not OS-level events.
- DDoS, port scans, brute force, web attacks produce the same flow patterns regardless of the target OS.
- **Limitation:** OS-specific attacks (e.g., EternalBlue SMB exploit) have unique payload signatures that flow-level analysis may not capture — this requires deep packet inspection (DPI), which is outside our scope.

### 11.2 "What file formats are supported?"

| Format | Supported | How |
|--------|-----------|-----|
| `.pcap` | ✅ | NFStream native support |
| `.pcapng` | ✅ | NFStream native support |
| `.pcap.bz2` | ✅ | Auto-decompressed before processing |
| CSV/Parquet | ✅ | Upload via `/upload-file` endpoint |
| Live traffic | ✅ | NFStream on any network interface |
| Syslog/JSON | ❌ | Would need a format adapter |

