## 14. RAPID-FIRE Q&A

**Q: Why not deep learning?**
A: Tree-based models outperform DL on tabular data (NeurIPS 2022 paper). RF is faster, more interpretable, and doesn't need GPU.

**Q: Is 99% accuracy real or overfitting?**
A: Validated on held-out test sets per attack type. Stress tests show 0% false positives on random noise. However, cross-dataset (IDS2018) drops to 50% — proving the model learns CICIDS2017 patterns specifically, not universal attack patterns. This is honest and expected.

**Q: How many flows can it handle?**
A: ~20 flows/sec on a single instance. Model inference is 5ms per flow. Bottleneck is TI API rate limits (cacheable).

**Q: Why MongoDB over SQL?**
A: Network logs are semi-structured with 70+ optional fields. Document model avoids constant schema migrations. Append-heavy write pattern suits MongoDB.

**Q: Why Grok and not ChatGPT?**
A: 30× cheaper, lower latency, OpenAI-compatible API (swappable). For FYP budget, Grok's free tier is sufficient.

**Q: What happens if the AI copilot gives wrong advice?**
A: Copilot is advisory only — no automated actions without human approval. Playbook steps are marked `auto: true/false` to distinguish automated vs human-review actions.

**Q: Can an attacker evade detection?**
A: Single-model evasion is possible. But evading RF + IF + TI simultaneously is exponentially harder. This is the core value of the consensus approach.

**Q: Why not Snort/Suricata rules?**
A: Rules detect known signatures only. Our ML detects behavioral patterns. Isolation Forest can flag zero-day attacks that have no existing signature.

**Q: What if the database goes down?**
A: MongoDB Atlas has automatic failover and backups. The API returns errors gracefully — no data loss in the ML pipeline.

**Q: Why flow-level and not packet-level analysis?**
A: Flow-level reduces data volume by 100-1000×, captures behavioral patterns (rates, durations), and matches CICIDS2017's feature format. Packet-level DPI is a privacy concern and computationally expensive.

**Q: How do you handle class imbalance?**
A: (1) `class_weight="balanced"` in RF, (2) Capping each class at 50K samples, (3) Stratified train/test split.

**Q: What's the attack type classifier based on?**
A: A rule-based post-processor that examines flow features (SYN flags, packet rates, durations) after ML scoring. When rules don't match but ML confidence is high, it falls back to ML-confirmed labels.

**Q: Why not use MITRE ATT&CK directly for classification?**
A: MITRE ATT&CK is a framework, not a classifier. We use AI (Grok) to map our detected alerts to MITRE techniques — bridging ML detection with established taxonomy.

